      <workList xml:id="workDesc_5fa2f2ad">
         <work xml:id="work_id251ff090">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_6c5da074">C1:Coll.001</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_ide9f049e1" xml:lang="de">Abhandlung über das Recitativ</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_ee5d7b0c"/>
            <creation xml:id="creation_id19aa248e">
               <date enddate="1765" startdate="1764" xml:id="date_7e16511f">1764–65</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_idbc0fa50c">
               <p xml:id="idm45">During the early 1760s Scheibe was carrying out a detailed study on the relations between declamation, recitative, aria, chorale and rhetoric, including rhythm and syllabic issues, and even emotions and instrumentation. Around that time, Scheibe was living in the house of his acquaintance, <persName xml:id="idm46" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Heinrich Wilhelm von Gerstenberg</persName>, and together they produced the cantata, ‘Ariadne auf Naxos’, which was published in 1765. Scheibe was also discussing the subject with the poet <persName xml:id="idm47" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Friedrich Gottlieb Klopstock</persName>, who was living in Copenhagen, though Klopstock found Scheibe rather obstinate. Scheibe had indeed been interested in the subject since his early days and wrote on it in his <rend xml:id="idm48" fontstyle="italic">Critischer Musikus</rend> (SchW C1:008); he also dealt with the subject in his response to a review of one of his works (1765; SchW C1:010) as well as in an extensive introduction to his <rend xml:id="idm49" fontstyle="italic">Tragische Cantaten</rend> (1765; SchW B2:Coll.001). The subject was indeed very popular among Scheibe’s contemporaries and <persName xml:id="idm50" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName>, with whom Scheibe collaborated in connection with his first edition of <rend xml:id="idm51" fontstyle="italic">Der Critische Musicus</rend> (1738–40) and with whom he also held a correspondence, advertised already in 1733 that he would be publishing a ‘Traité du recitatif’. Also <persName xml:id="idm52" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Heinrich Bokemeyer</persName> whom Scheibe knew wrote on syllabic issues. Telemann and <persName xml:id="idm53" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Carl Heinrich Graun</persName>, whose works Scheibe greatly admired, discuss these issues in their correspondence during the 1750s, so it seems likely that Scheibe and Graun also may have discussed the subject. <persName xml:id="idm54" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Gottfried Heinrich Stölzel</persName>, a contemporary of Scheibe’s, also wrote a treatise on the recitative which, however, remained in manuscript. Later <persName xml:id="idm55" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Georg Sulzer</persName> based some of the articles in his <rend xml:id="idm56" fontstyle="italic">Allgemeine Theorie</rend> (1779) on Scheibe’s studies. The first part of the ‘Abhandlung über das Recitativ’ appeared in 1764 while the two remaining parts were issued the following year. The articles not only include a thorough discussion of the subject but also extensive analyses of various musical works that Scheibe had composed (SchW B1:001, B2:301, B2:432, B2:435, B2:439). The three articles are mentioned in a review of <rend xml:id="idm57" fontstyle="italic">Tragische Kantaten</rend> which appeared in <rend xml:id="idm58" fontstyle="italic">Bibliothek der schönen Wissenschaften</rend> (1765): 'In both cantatas he [i.e. Scheibe] mainly shows the distinction between recitation and declamation and holds that the former belongs only to the actual recitative and cannot be found in other musical styles of writing; the latter, however, will be required in recitatives as well as in arias and all other types of vocal music, and that even instrumental music when it needs expression cannot escape it [i.e. declamation] but must indeed be enlivened by it. Hence he explains the two main characters of the recitative which derive from it, namely of the recitative kind [‘recitativischen’] and of the declamatory – these have been treated extensively by us in the issued study at the appropriate place. Finally, he shows how he has proceeded in the arrangement of these cantatas, and in the afterword provides further instructions both for the keyboard player and singer on what they need to observe in the performance of them' (p. 345).</p>
            </history>
